    "details": "Patrick Arminio reports:\n\n> \\\"PermissionExtension.resolve()\\\" evaluates the return value of\n> \\\"has_permission()\\\" for truthiness on the synchronous path.\n> \\\"supports_sync\\\" only classifies a permission as asynchronous when\n> \\\"has_permission\\\" is declared with \\\"async def\\\" (via\n> \\\"inspect.iscoroutinefunction\\\"), so a plain \\\"def\\\" that returns an\n> awaitable is treated as synchronous. An awaitable is always truthy, so\n> the check passes even when it resolves to \\\"False\\\" and the protected\n> resolver runs. The resolve path is chosen by the field resolver, not\n> by the execution method, so any field with a synchronous resolver is\n> affected under both \\\"execute_sync()\\\" and \\\"execute()\\\". Permissions\n> declared with \\\"async def has_permission()\\\", or a plain \\\"def\\\"\n> returning a boolean, are not affected.\n",
    "id": "FreeBSD-2026-0438",
